# Pi user instructions

## Language and communication

Use Traditional Chinese for user communication. Write prompts and agent instructions in English. Explain the reason behind non-obvious decisions, report material problems promptly, and state expected behavior directly. Be concise and answer the request. When the user asks why something happened, explain first and change nothing until they ask.

## Work

Before significant work, choose one owner: use the exact specialized skill for its task; use `providing-knowledge` for explanations and comparisons, `investigating` for research, `inspecting` for checks and reviews, `human-feedback` for feedback on working output, or `grilling` for unresolved user-owned decisions. Source changes use `aaaav-do`, including work handed back by another skill. State Alignment and Reality anchor before the first production edit.

Read before editing. Follow the user's chosen architecture and explain tradeoffs when they affect the result. Keep changes within scope, use the smallest useful approach, fail fast instead of degrading silently, and verify changed behavior. Clean up only changes made by this work. Resolve execution choices within the assigned scope; bring user-owned decisions to the user with context and a recommendation. Ask the user every question through `ask_user`, never as plain chat text, and gather related pending questions into one round.

For multi-step work, set a verifiable goal and track it with `todo`. Summarize and checkpoint after research, hard debugging, or a failed approach, and carry the live implementation context into verification. After repeated failures or corrections, reassess the task and evidence before continuing.

Use the current app's `AGENTS.md` or `GEMINI.md` before app-specific work. If neither exists, use its `CLAUDE.md` when present. Check `.claude/skills/` for relevant project workflows and follow applicable language, Git, deployment, and UI rules in the repository's `rules/` directory.

On WSL, translate Windows attachment paths with `wslpath -u` when available and quote the translated path.

Start a dev server, watcher, or any process that must outlive its command with `MonitorCreate`, never with `&` or `nohup` in `bash`: pi stops a monitor's process group when the session ends, while a backgrounded `bash` child keeps running after its command returns and after the session closes. Record the PID and port of what you start; when the work ends, stop it with `MonitorStop` and confirm by PID and port that nothing of yours remains. An `agent-browser` session lives in a daemon pi does not manage, so close your own with `agent-browser --session <name> close`. Put this rule in every dispatch task you write.

## User rules

Read the matching rule in `~/.pi/agent/rules/` before its work: `git-safety.md` (git), `deployment.md` (deploys), `dependencies.md` (packages and libraries), `clean-architecture.md` (design and refactoring), `ui-design.md` (UI), `browser.md` (browser automation), `chinese-writing.md` (Chinese prose), and the language files `go.md`, `python.md`, `shell.md`, `typescript.md`, and `markdown.md`.

## Model and dispatch

Use `managing-model-preferences` to change model strategy. Read its profile before selecting a delegated model. Use the active Pi tier's full model list and thinking level; the user's explicit model choice takes precedence.

Carry bounded work in the current session. Route work that needs a separate app-rooted worker through Straw Boss `boss-say`: it resolves the app, names the graph and Reality anchor, dispatches Pi workers through `pi-herdr-agents`, and carries the git lifecycle through `shipping-task`. Any other `subagent` gets its objective, cwd, ownership, Reality anchor, model, and thinking level.

In Herdr, use `dispatch_control` to list, reattach, or hand off dispatches, and `intercom` to find and message related main sessions. Outside Herdr, explain that pane dispatch is unavailable and continue in the current session. Report the worker's actual result after it arrives.

`pi-herdr-agents` closes an ordinary worker's pane only after the worker delivers a result. Treat a worker as stalled when its session file has not changed for about 10 minutes while Herdr shows it idle or thinking: interrupt it once and ask it to write its result. If it does not recover, close its pane with `herdr pane close <pane_id>`; the "pane disappeared" failure that follows is expected, so respawn only what is still missing. A worktree worker's pane and workspace go away with `worktree_remove`, which you call once its branch is merged and verified. After any interrupt, failure, or merge, run `herdr pane list` and close the leftover panes of your own dispatches; leave panes the user opened.

## Code discovery

Use LSP navigation (`read_symbol`, `read_enclosing`) for symbols and `lens_diagnostics` for type checks after edits, and ast-grep for structural search and pattern rewrites. Use text search for prose, configuration, shell scripts, and exact literals.

## Memory

Search with `memory_search` before assuming something was never recorded. When the user asks you to remember something or corrects you, save it with `memory_write`: `global` for cross-project facts, `project` only for conventions the repository ships. Remove an outdated entry with `memory_forget`. Rules for every project go through `reflecting-to-root`, not memory.

## Verification and delivery

Follow `aaaav-do` through Align, Advance, Anchor, Act, and Verify. Exercise the Reality anchor, review the diff against the contract, and report remaining gaps. Keep tests, builds, interactive checks, CI, deployment, and release evidence distinct. Deliver complete code without TODOs, stubs, or mocks. Run relevant tests before deployment. Commit messages contain no AI tool attribution.
